WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.669
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Jeffrey Mandel, who's president and lead

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:09.230
counsel at Law Forward, the firm that filed
the landmark lawsuit against the fraudulent

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:12.370
2020 electors in Wisconsin.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:13.370
Really great having you on.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:14.370
I appreciate your time.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:15.950
Speaker 5: Thanks so much, David.

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:16.950
It's great to be here.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:22.390
Speaker 1: So just to get everybody kind of
up to speed, we saw attempts at this fake

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:27.599
elector scam in a number of different states,
Arizona's one place where it made a lot of

00:00:27.599 --> 00:00:28.599
news.

00:00:28.599 --> 00:00:32.189
The concept was even though Joe Biden won.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:33.280
What if we.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.860
We meaning typically some Republicans.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:40.910
What if we just send Republican electors to
say we're giving our electoral votes to Donald

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:42.550
Trump anyway?

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:43.550
That's the gist of it.

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:44.550
Is that right?

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.010
Speaker 5: That is the gist of it.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:51.129
It requires us stepping back and sort of thinking
back to Schoolhouse Rock and our basic understanding

00:00:51.129 --> 00:00:57.390
of, sir, we all think that every fourth November
we go to the polls and we vote for a candidate

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.390
for president.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:00:59.390
Right.

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:01.350
People say, did you vote for Biden or did
you vote for Trump?

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:06.159
But in reality, you're voting for a slate
of electors who will represent your state

00:01:06.159 --> 00:01:07.220
in the Electoral College.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:09.430
It is not the popular vote.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:12.460
It is that Electoral College vote that chooses
the president.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:19.960
And so what we had was seven states where
even though the popular vote said that the

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:24.670
electors pledged to Joe Biden and Kamala Harris
would represent that state in the Electoral

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:26.170
College.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:32.299
Nonetheless, electors who were pledged to
Donald Trump and Mike Pence chose to meet

00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:39.420
and submit papers claiming that they were
actually the valid, qualified and duly elected

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.210
electors for their state.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:46.770
So in Wisconsin, for example, what that meant
was we had ten individuals and as you note

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:54.219
there, mostly Republican Party bigwigs who
decided that they knew better than 3.3 million

00:01:54.219 --> 00:01:59.380
of their neighbors, a record 3.3 million Wisconsinites
voted in 2020.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:03.939
And these ten people decided that they didn't
care what those other 3.3 million people thought

00:02:03.939 --> 00:02:09.080
they were going to take it upon themselves
to hijack Wisconsin's participation in choosing

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.210
the next president.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:16.390
Speaker 1: Now, when we talk about the January
six committee, there's an investigation happening

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:21.500
in Georgia, etc., the question of criminality
of some of the different things that were

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.510
attempted is often brought up, to be clear.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.410
You have filed a lawsuit that seeks damages.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:30.680
The criminality question is a different one
when it comes to these schemes.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:31.680
Is that right?

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:32.720
Speaker 5: That's right.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:38.870
And I do think that at least here under Wisconsin
state law and around the country, under federal

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:44.440
law, I think that there is a likelihood that
many of these people committed crimes, crimes

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:48.650
and could be prosecuted and potentially put
in jail.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:56.190
But what we're filing is a civil lawsuit that
seeks civil remedies and will be a way of

00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:00.760
trying to learn more about exactly what did
happen here in Wisconsin and how it worked.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:05.860
Because a civil lawsuit comes with discovery,
the opportunity to see document is to take

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.100
depositions and have testimony.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:12.820
Speaker 1: I'm trying to think back there
was this kind of circus tour that went around

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:18.240
the country that involved Rudy Giuliani and
Jenna Ellis and sometimes Sidney Powell and

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.360
others.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.910
Did did that circus go through Wisconsin around
the same time that the fake elector scam was

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:26.330
being kind of conceived?

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:32.620
Speaker 5: Well, the circus never came fully
to town here in Wisconsin.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:37.050
Rudy Giuliani, for example, to my knowledge,
never came to Wisconsin.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:40.660
We did have one of Sidney Powells crackin
lawsuits.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:41.660
Yes.

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:44.320
And conspiracy theories here in Wisconsin.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.602
And what's interesting about that, I mean,
there are a lot of things that are interesting

00:03:47.602 --> 00:03:48.602
about that.

00:03:48.602 --> 00:03:51.840
But one of the things that's interesting about
that is the plaintiff that Sidney Powell represented

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.770
in that lawsuit is one of the fraudulent electors
right here in Wisconsin.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:59.030
So all of this is tied together.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:02.530
And so the circus came to town through the
courts, but it never came to town with the

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:03.670
with the full media entourage.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:07.330
Speaker 1: So in terms of your lawsuit, I
mean, what are you alleging?

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:08.330
What are the damages?

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:09.330
Who.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.270
Who was damaged, so to speak?

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:12.270
Speaker 5: Sure.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.260
So we represent three plaintiffs.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:20.010
Two of them were actually the among the valid
electors for Joe Biden and Kamala Harris.

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:21.010
Okay.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:26.070
One of them is a voter who feels strongly
that these people tried to disenfranchize

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:30.650
her and steal the value of her vote by going
against the will of the voters.

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:37.350
And what we're doing is we're using some old
established common law ideas to try to get

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:38.450
at this conduct.

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:44.050
Fundamentally, the problem is, nobody ever
thought that someone would try something as

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:48.170
audacious as what these fake electors did
in these seven states.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:52.640
So there is no law that says you can't do
that because we don't pass laws that tell

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.220
people not to do things that nobody would
ever imagine someone would do.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:59.410
And that has led to this challenge of saying.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:04.440
No matter who we talk to, when we explain
what the fraudulent electors did, everyone

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:05.700
says, Oh, that can't be right.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.520
That can't be legal.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:13.300
That offends my most basic sense of fair play
and civic understanding of democracy.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:14.300
Right.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:15.710
But there isn't a specific law.

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:20.850
So what we've done is we've taken some very
old principles from the common law and said

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.060
that we think that these fit.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:25.730
So one of them is public nuisance, right?

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:33.890
Our election system and our democracy is a
finely calibrated and somewhat fragile structure

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:35.930
that is for the public good.

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:42.710
And there is a long standing idea that if
if I take private action that harms the public

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:47.950
good, someone can try to stop me using a doctrine
that says that I'm creating a nuisance to

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:51.390
the public for my own benefit, but everyone
else's detriment.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:53.470
So we're using that theory.

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:59.790
We're using a theory of civil conspiracy that
these ten electors and thus far, we've also

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:03.200
named two lawyers who assisted them.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:06.830
And we might need to add more people as we
find out who else was involved.

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:07.830
All right.

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:12.820
The January 6th committee recently made quite
clear that Senator Johnson seems to be involved,

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:20.190
but that these people conspired to break the
law, whether they ultimately did break the

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:21.190
law.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:22.190
Right.

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:23.190
That's sort of the criminal question.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:25.700
But the fact that they were trying to break
the law, that they were trying to convince

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:30.710
Congress to take these papers, that in itself,
we think is actionable.

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:34.190
And there is a long, long standing idea.

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:38.250
And I apologize for the Latin, but it goes
under the name of quo work.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:44.540
And so this is a writ that an order that you
could go to for hundreds of years to court

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:47.510
if you were supposed to serve in a public
office.

00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:51.150
But someone else was occupying that office
and keeping you out of it.

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:58.540
And so we are bringing a claim for such an
order on behalf of the actual electors that

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:01.210
these other people have tried to usurp that
office.

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:06.200
And by saying we trump fraudulent electors,
we are the real electors, they're implicitly

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:11.820
saying that the Biden electors are liars and
are not the real electors.

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:13.950
And so we're also bringing a claim under under
that.

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:19.410
Speaker 1: You have the have the have the
fraudulent electors had to personally lawyer

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:20.660
up as a result of this.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:21.660
At this point.

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:22.800
Speaker 5: They have.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:29.010
So they've all hired counsel and were in those
early stages of lawsuits, as you know, David.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:30.610
Litigation can be kind of slow.

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:31.610
Yes.

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:34.639
So we're in the early stages of the lawyers
sort of hoovering.

00:07:34.639 --> 00:07:36.340
We filed in state court.

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:39.870
They would rather be federal court, which
is not where this case belongs.

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:42.100
And so we're in the process of bringing it
back to state court.

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:46.800
Speaker 1: What kind of damages and restitution
are you seeking here?

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:49.070
Speaker 5: So we're seeking a number of things.

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:54.490
First, we're seeking a clear and unambiguous
order from a court that what happened here

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:58.810
was improper and unlawful and it should never
happen again.

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:03.320
Second, we are seeking an order that would
prohibit any of these people from ever being

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:07.780
involved with the Electoral College in Wisconsin
again, because it seems to me that they have

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:12.580
abused the privilege of being part of our
civic fabric, and they should they should

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:15.930
not have that opportunity again, aggrieved.

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:20.480
Third, we are seeking deterrence.

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:22.890
We want accountability and we want deterrence.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:24.900
We never want to see this happen again.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:30.270
And so we are seeking punitive damages for
the fact that these folks deliberately interfered

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:33.099
in the legal rights of the real electors.

00:08:33.099 --> 00:08:37.680
And under Wisconsin law, those punitive damages
could be up to $200,000 for each of the defendants.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:43.130
Speaker 1: Have you been in touch with potential
with lawyers that are potentially looking

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:46.140
at doing this in other states where these
schemes were hatched?

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:50.090
Speaker 5: We've certainly we've certainly
had some conversations and some outreach.

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:51.240
It is my hope.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:54.910
I really believe that both accountability
and deterrence are essential so this doesn't

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:56.000
happen again.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:59.570
And so it is my hope that we will see cases
like this in all of the states.

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:05.920
Speaker 1: One of the big questions is if
whoever had followed through on this, like

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:10.421
if these electors had gotten on a plane or,
you know, whatever the real attempt was, a

00:09:10.421 --> 00:09:13.010
bona fide attempt, what would have happened?

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:18.660
Like could could they have said, no, we believe
that Trump gets these votes.

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:19.830
And then what would have happened?

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:23.980
I mean, John Eastman wrote this memo of what
Pence could do.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:25.870
He's now in legal jeopardy himself.

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:27.080
He's had the lawyer up.

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:28.160
The FBI recently seized his phone.

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:31.220
You know, he's got his fair share of problems
for even suggesting this framework.

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:35.520
But there's this question of like, if they
both show up, then then what would have happened?

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:39.430
Speaker 5: Well, I mean, the reality is we
came a lot closer to that then than we'd like

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:45.060
to think, because I think your intuition that
the Electoral College happens in Washington

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:48.120
is a common one, but that's actually not right.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:53.460
The Electoral College happens in each state's
capital at the same time, on the same date.

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:59.540
And so when these fraudulent electors in Madison
on December 14th of 20.

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:03.610
They and they signed these papers claiming
to be the real electors.

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:08.420
And when that happened in Arizona and in Georgia
and in Michigan and in Nevada and in Pennsylvania

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:12.120
and in New Mexico, that that was the thing.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:13.670
Speaker 1: They submitted these paper.

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:14.670
That's the act.

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:19.020
In other words, like the real electors did
it on that day and the fake electors did.

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:20.920
And then so at that point, it's.

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:22.140
But they've both done it.

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:23.690
Speaker 5: That's absolutely right.

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:25.741
And it really all came down to two.

00:10:25.741 --> 00:10:33.060
Then Vice President Pence, if he had followed
John Eastman's wishes and been willing to

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:37.660
credit those votes, then we would have seen
the chaos.

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:39.380
We would have seen how Congress reacted.

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:45.820
And we've seen almost certainly litigation,
but we really would have been in a constitutional

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:46.890
crisis.

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:55.960
It is, to Mr. Pence's credit that he refused
to go along with that and recognize that in

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:59.380
our in our democracy, the will of the voters
really is sacrosanct.

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:03.910
Speaker 1: Yet Thom Hartmann, radio host Thom
Hartmann has an interesting Twitter thread

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:05.170
from in the last week.

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:09.670
It might be a few days ago where he kind of
outlines.

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:17.040
What this could look like in 2024 with the
difference that there is a move to accept

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:22.770
different electors, and that although we all
intuitively know that's not right, it's not

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:26.230
what's supposed to happen, it would be a violation
of the will of the people.

00:11:26.230 --> 00:11:31.920
All of the things that you've said, it's not
so cut and dry that it obviously fails, that

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:35.250
it could get pretty complicated and dicey
for a period of time.

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:37.480
Speaker 5: It absolutely could.

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:44.120
And one of the ways that that could happen
is if state courts that support the losing

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:48.899
candidate wanted to sort of muddy the waters.

00:11:48.899 --> 00:11:54.399
In Wisconsin, for example, the we had a partial
recount.

00:11:54.399 --> 00:11:59.640
President Trump's campaign asked for a recount
in in the Madison area and Milwaukee.

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:03.430
And then when they didn't like the results
of that recount, that actually showed that

00:12:03.430 --> 00:12:06.190
that Biden won by more votes.

00:12:06.190 --> 00:12:10.300
They they they filed a lawsuit, as they're
entitled to do, to challenge the results of

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:11.440
the recount.

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:14.230
And that case went up to the Wisconsin Supreme
Court.

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:16.860
The Wisconsin Supreme Court took unprecedented
actions.

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:19.110
I don't think this had ever happened before.

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.660
The case came in on a Friday afternoon.

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:27.610
The Electoral College was meeting on Monday
at noon, and that's a statutory deadline.

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:31.459
And so the court had people brief late at
night on Friday night.

00:12:31.459 --> 00:12:38.990
They held oral argument by Zoom on Saturday,
and they issued a full opinion with all of

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:43.310
its reasoning, everything on Monday morning,
so that they would have given the final word

00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:47.600
on the election results in Wisconsin before
the Electoral College met.

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:52.750
But imagine, I mean, Mr. Eastman's arguments
would be much stronger if the court had not

00:12:52.750 --> 00:12:53.810
done that.

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:59.089
If there was still a question about who the
legal winner in a state was then.

00:12:59.089 --> 00:13:03.050
There is a much better argument for these
dueling slates.

00:13:03.050 --> 00:13:06.560
And you really have questions then about how
Congress is supposed to resolve this or what

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:07.870
they're supposed to do.

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:16.000
So we could with what we've learned from 2020,
we could imagine in 2024, a number of actors

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:22.100
taking making specific decisions to try to
leave more flexibility for Congress or the

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:27.899
vice president or make this uglier and harder
in ways that are really, really dangerous

00:13:27.899 --> 00:13:28.899
to our democracy.

00:13:28.899 --> 00:13:30.560
Speaker 1: Very, very scary.

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:34.580
But one piece of what is being done is this
lawsuit.

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:40.000
And we've been speaking with Jeffrey Mandel,
president and lead counsel at Law Forward.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:43.440
Thanks so much for talking to me today and
explaining what you're what you're doing.

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:45.211
Speaker 5: Thanks, David, for having me.

00:13:45.211 --> 00:13:46.211
It's a pleasure.

00:13:46.211 --> 00:13:49.810
I appreciate your attention to this and I
hope we can come back and update you as it

00:13:49.810 --> 00:13:50.000
moves forward.

